// Timestamps off by the configuration, the peer offering SACK-permitted and window scaling: the SYN-ACK answers in
// Linux's twelve-byte layout, nop, nop, sackOK, nop, wscale. A full segment is the whole 1460 bytes, so the budget is
// 512 * 1460 / 2 = 373760, and the first ACK states it less the 1000 unread bytes in units of 8:
// (373761 - 1001) / 8 = 46595. Linux with net.ipv4.tcp_timestamps=0 lays the options out the same way.
--tcp_timestamps=0
0 socket(..., SOCK_STREAM, IPPROTO_TCP) = 3
+0 bind(3, ..., ...) = 0
+0 listen(3, 1) = 0

+0 < S 0:0(0) win 65535 <mss 1460,nop,nop,sackOK,nop,wscale 7>
+0 > S. 0:0(0) ack 1 win 65535 <mss 1460,nop,nop,sackOK,nop,wscale 3>
+0 < . 1:1(0) ack 1 win 512
+0 accept(3, ..., ...) = 4

+0 < P. 1:1001(1000) ack 1 win 512
+0 > . 1:1(0) ack 1001 win 46595
